    public DocumentDefinition Create() => DocumentDefinition.Create(ScribeNode.Create(
        "Uniform signed squares in the original algebra", H("Uniform signed squares in the original algebra"), Blocks(
        Paragraph(Text("Let A be an arbitrary nontrivial C*-algebra in Type u with PartialOrder A and StarOrderedRing A. Let beta be a real linear positive normalized functional on selfAdjoint A, extreme in the whole original real state space. For every original self-adjoint k with beta(k) nonzero, one finite nonnegative L is chosen before every positive eta. There is an original self-adjoint e of norm at most L. Define square(e) as the self-adjoint subtype of e squared, sigma(r)=1 for positive r and minus one otherwise, and c=sigma(beta(k)) times square(e). The element c is self-adjoint, sigma(beta(k)) times c is nonnegative in A, its original norm is at most L squared, and sqrt(beta(square(c-k))) is less than eta. Both signs are included. There is no finite-dimensional, sequential or separability restriction. This assertion concerns C*-algebras; general JB algebras and the further Jordan-algebra conclusion are separate questions. The construction first forms a positive rank-one operator from pi(k)Omega, then its self-adjoint square root T. The bound L=1+norm(T) precedes the accuracy. Strong self-adjoint density and the original-A truncation e=L times cfc(f,a), f(t)=2t/(1+t squared), give the original norm bound and simultaneous approximation on Omega and T(Omega). Contractivity controls the squared actions. The exact GNS moment identity identifies the original beta square reading with the represented vector norm.")),
        Describe.Lean(DescribeId.Create("statement0"), DeclarationHandle.Create("D5/S3/Quantum/Algebra/StateApproximation/UniformSignedSquare.sigma_sq"),
